-- ============================================================================
-- E2E ANCHOR SEED — replicates the frontend/e2e suite's pinned anchors on the
-- Flyway-built test schema, so the FULL data-backed Playwright suite can run in
-- CI against a plain gvenzl/oracle-free container instead of the real-data
-- seeded image.
--
-- LOCATION MATTERS: this file lives in db-e2e/, NOT db/. The backend *IT suite
-- (AbstractOracleIT, locations "classpath:db") must NEVER load it — several ITs
-- assert exact list contents (the SBS biogeo prefix-search order in V28, the
-- reporting-years list in V8, mills-endpoint counts), and the rows below would
-- break those assertions. Only the CI e2e job's Flyway run includes this
-- location:
--   mvn -P e2e-db flyway:clean flyway:migrate   (see backend/pom.xml, the
--   e2e-db profile appends filesystem:src/test/resources/db-e2e)
--
-- SOURCE OF TRUTH: every (mill, year, state) here is transcribed from the
-- pinned-anchor documentation in frontend/e2e/fixtures/*/**-test-data.ts and
-- from EVERY file under frontend/e2e/real-test-data-patches/ (CI has no sqlplus
-- step, so a patch that is not folded in here simply does not exist in CI). The
-- same (mill, year) pairs exist with identical states in the real-data seeded
-- image, so the UNMODIFIED e2e suite passes against either database. When an
-- e2e fixture is re-grounded or a patch adds data, update this file in the SAME
-- change.
--
-- THAT PARITY CLAIM IS ABOUT THE ANCHORS, NOT THE WHOLE MILL-YEAR — one known
-- counter-example, so nobody reads it as stronger than it is. In the extract,
-- 25052/2016 (sch1's delete target) also carries a Schedule 3 with a Crown
-- Timber volume; here it does not. Schedule 1 pre-fills its volume codes from
-- that value when nothing is saved (BR-09), so its served document differs
-- between the two databases even though both are Draft, openable and identical
-- in every report-status respect. That cost a real red — sch1 defects.md VER-1.
-- Before leaning on a mill-year's NEIGHBOURING schedules, check both databases.
--
-- THAT RULE IS MACHINE-ENFORCED, not a convention:
-- frontend/e2e/preflight/ci-seed-parity.setup.ts reads this file and the six
-- fixture files and fails the run when a pinned anchor is neither seeded here
-- nor listed in its DELIBERATELY_ABSENT map. It runs in the `setup` project on
-- every local and CI run, needs no database, and checks BOTH directions — a
-- guard anchor whose whole fixture is the ABSENCE of a row (see below) fails
-- that gate if someone "helpfully" seeds it.
--
-- TRANSCRIBE, DON'T PASTE. The patches are guarded PL/SQL against a POPULATED
-- schema; this file is plain INSERTs with pre-claimed ids against an EMPTY one.
-- Copying a patch's DECLARE/IF-NOT-EXISTS block in here would work and would
-- also make the id claims below unverifiable, which is the whole point of them.
--
-- ORDERING NOTE (2026-08-28, Story 5.7). Flyway orders repeatables by
-- description, so db/R__70_test_scope_canonical_submitter.sql runs BEFORE this
-- file. Its set-based INSERT associates its canonical submitter with every
-- ILCR_MILL_STATUS_XREF row that exists AT THAT MOMENT — which excludes every
-- mill below, because they are added here, afterwards. That is harmless today:
-- the e2e job runs ILCR_SECURITY_ENABLED=false, and MillContextService
-- .validateMillAccess returns early when the principal is not a Jwt (:85-87), so
-- mill-scope enforcement is a no-op for this suite. It stops being harmless the
-- day the e2e job runs security-ON — every anchor below would then 403 with
-- "Mill is not associated to the caller." on all ~268 data-backed scenarios at
-- once, which reads as an authorization regression rather than as missing
-- fixture rows.
--
-- TRACKED as bcgov/nr-ilcr#385, so the fix does not rest on someone reading this
-- comment at the right moment. The fix is to add the xref rows HERE (this file
-- already runs after R__70, so the ILCR_USER row its FK needs exists) rather
-- than renumbering either file — #385 records why renumbering was rejected.
-- Note the trigger is NOT a flag flip: the suite has no way to present a real
-- principal today, so #385 is best sequenced with whatever gives it one.
--
-- NOT REPLICATED, deliberately: THE.ILCR_REPORT_CATEGORY. Three of the patches
-- seed eleven category rows per anchor because the REAL Oracle has a composite
-- FK onto that table and a bare mill-year 500s on its first save
-- (DataIntegrityViolationException). The Flyway test schema models the table
-- (db/V20260819__seed_reporting_year_open_fixtures.sql) with a PK and NO FK,
-- and no schedule 1/2/3/4/11 code path reads it (only reportingyear and
-- schedule5 do), so those rows change nothing here. The omission is a decision,
-- not an oversight.
--
-- ID CLAIMS (all verified free against every migration in db/ on 2026-08-24;
-- re-verified for the ranges extended on 2026-08-28):
--   MILL_ID / ILCR_MILL_STATUS_XREF_ID : 1, 13, 9050, 10050, 12050, 13050,
--                                        16050, 17052, 22050, 22051, 23050,
--                                        23051, 23052, 24050, 24051,
--                                        25050-25054
--   ILCR_REPORTING_PERIOD              : 2015-2019 (2020/2021 exist in V2/V8)
--   ILCR_REPORT_SUMMARY_ID             : 3001-3044   (db/ uses 1001-1228)
--   ILCR_COST_REPORT_DETAIL_ID         : 4001-4305   (db/ uses 5001-9506)
--   TRANSPORTATION_REPORT_ID           : 4801-4808
--   BASIC_SILVICULTURE_REPORT_ID       : 9351-9358
--   BIOGEOCLIMATIC_CATALOGUE_ID        : 40, 171, 8850-8855
-- All explicit ids sit below the runtime sequences (ILCR_REPORT_COMMON_SEQ
-- >= 9500, ILCR_COST_REPORT_DETAIL_SEQ >= 10000), so app-created rows never
-- collide with these.
-- ============================================================================

